.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Config 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Config
.Nd the OpenBSD-style configuration grammar
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::Config;

my $config = Fugu::Config->new(file => '/etc/mydaemon.conf');
$config->load or die $config->error . "\en";

my $port = $config->get('port', 8080);

for my $device ($config->blocks('device')) {
    say $device->{settings}{name};
}

my $vm = $config->block('vm', 'default');
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
parses the configuration grammar that
.Ox
daemons use.
A file holds top-level settings and blocks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
log_level debug
hap_port = 51827

device tasmota thermostat bedroom {
	name = "Bedroom Thermostat"
	topic = tasmota_AABBCC
}
.Ed
.Pp
Both
.Ql key value
and
.Ql key = value
parse.
A double-quoted value loses its quotes.
A
.Ql #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
.Pp
A block header is a type, then one or more arguments, then an opening
brace.
Blocks nest one level; a block inside a block is an error.
.Pp
The parser keeps the blocks of each type in file order, and also
addresses each one by its name, which is the last argument of the
header.
Thus a caller that wants an ordered list of accessories and a caller
that wants the machine called
.Dq default
read the same parse.
.Pp
A malformed line is an error with a file and a line number.
The module never skips a line it did not understand.
A typo that a parser ignores is a setting that silently does not
apply, and the operator finds out from the behavior of the daemon
weeks later.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a parser for one file.
The method does not read the file.
Call
.Fn load .
.Pp
This is the argument:
.Bl -tag -width "fileXX"
.It Fa file
The configuration file.
This argument is necessary.
.El
.Ss load
.Fn load
reads and parses the file.
A second call starts over, so a setting that the file no longer holds
is gone.
.Pp
An unreadable file is an error.
A caller for whom an absent file is normal tests for the file first.
A daemon that silently runs on defaults after a typo in a path is
worse than one that refuses to start.
.Ss file
.Fn file
returns the path that this object parses.
.Ss error
.Fn error
returns the most recent failure, with its file and line.
.Ss get
.Fn get "$key" "$default"
returns a top-level setting, or the default.
.Ss setting_names
.Fn setting_names
returns the names of the top-level settings, sorted.
.Ss bool
.Fn bool "$key" "$default"
returns a top-level setting as a switch.
.Pp
The accepted spellings are
.Cm yes ,
.Cm true ,
.Cm on
and
.Cm 1 ,
and their opposites
.Cm no ,
.Cm false ,
.Cm off
and
.Cm 0 .
The comparison ignores case.
.Pp
Any other value returns the default and records an error.
Thus a caller that checks gets told, and a caller that does not still
gets a defined answer instead of the opposite of what the file said.
.Ss parse_bool
.Fn parse_bool "$value" "$default"
applies the same switch grammar to a value that the caller already
holds.
A setting inside a block comes through here.
.Ss blocks
.Fn blocks "$type"
returns every block of the type, in file order.
.Pp
Each entry is a hash reference with these keys:
.Bl -tag -width "settingsXX"
.It Va type
The block type.
.It Va args
The header arguments, in order, as an array reference.
.It Va name
The last header argument.
.It Va settings
The settings inside the block, as a hash reference.
.El
.Ss block
.Fn block "$type" "$name"
returns the block of the type whose name matches, or
.Dv undef .
When two blocks share a name, the last one in the file wins, as an
assignment does.
.Ss block_types
.Fn block_types
returns the block types that the file used, sorted.
.Ss find_project_root
.Fn find_project_root "$marker"
walks up from the working directory to the first directory that holds
.Fa $marker .
The method returns that directory, or
.Dv undef
when the walk reaches the root without a match.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn load
returns the object on success.
It returns
.Dv undef
when the file does not open or holds a line that the grammar does not
accept, with the reason in
.Fn error .
.Pp
.Fn get ,
.Fn file
and
.Fn error
return a string or
.Dv undef .
.Fn bool
and
.Fn parse_bool
return 1 or 0.
.Fn blocks ,
.Fn setting_names
and
.Fn block_types
return a list.
.Fn block
returns a hash reference or
.Dv undef .
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example loads a file that may not exist yet:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $config = Fugu::Config->new(file => $path);
if (-f $path) {
    $config->load or die $config->error . "\en";
}

my $port = $config->get('port', 8080);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies when
.Fa file
is absent or empty, and
.Fn find_project_root
dies without a marker.
Both are programming errors.
.Pp
No other method dies.
.Fn load
reports every parse failure through its return value and
.Fn error .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr Fugu::File 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Store 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The grammar has no escape for the comment character.
A value cannot hold a
.Ql # ,
even inside quotes.
.Pp
Every value is a string.
The module does not convert a number, and
.Ql 0
as a string is true in Perl.
Use
.Fn bool
for a switch.
.Pp
A key with no value is not a setting, and the parser rejects the line.
The grammar has no flag form.
